<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "Habitat",
      intro: "Elephants live across a wide range of landscapes in Africa and Asia. Wherever they are, they need plenty of food, " \
             "daily access to water and room to roam." %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Where on Earth are elephants found?</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Africa</h3>
      <p>
        African savanna elephants range across 23 countries south of the Sahara, from Kenya and Tanzania down to Botswana,
        Zimbabwe and South Africa. African forest elephants live in the rainforests of Central and West Africa,
        including Gabon, the Republic of the Congo and Cameroon.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Asia</h3>
      <p>
        Asian elephants live in 13 countries across South and Southeast Asia, including India, Sri Lanka, Nepal, Thailand,
        Myanmar, Malaysia and the islands of Sumatra and Borneo. India is home to more than half of all wild Asian elephants.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure class="photo-block">
    <%= render "shared/image_placeholder", label: "Range map coming soon" %>
    <figcaption>Where elephants live in Africa and Asia today.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>In the ground or in a tree?</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">In the ground?</span>
      <span class="fact-value">
        No. Elephants don't burrow or live underground. They do dig, though: they use their feet, trunks and tusks to reach
        water under dry riverbeds and to get at mineral-rich soil, which they eat for salt.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">In a tree?</span>
      <span class="fact-value">
        Definitely not. Weighing several tonnes, elephants live entirely on the ground. Trees are food, shade and scratching
        posts, and elephants often knock them over to reach the leaves at the top.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>What do elephants need to thrive?</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Water every day</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Elephants drink, bathe and wallow daily, so they rarely stray far from rivers, lakes or watering holes.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Lots of vegetation</span>
      <span class="fact-value">An adult eats over 100 kg of plants a day, so it needs large areas of grass, shrubs and trees.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Shade</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Trees and forest cover help elephants stay cool during the hottest part of the day.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Space to move</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Herds follow the seasons, travelling between wet- and dry-season feeding grounds along routes called corridors.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Minerals</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Salt licks and mineral-rich soils add nutrients that plants alone don't provide.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Where do elephants make their home?</h2>
  <div class="info-block">
    <p>
      Elephants don't build nests or dens. Their home is a <em>home range</em>, an area the family group knows well and moves
      through over the year. A home range can be under 100 km² where food and water are plentiful, or more than 10,000 km²
      in deserts such as Namibia's Kunene region.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>Did you know?</strong> The matriarch's memory of where to find water during droughts is key to her family's survival.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Other habitat facts</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Many landscapes</h3>
      <p>Savannas, grasslands, rainforests, swamps, mountain forests and even deserts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Ecosystem engineers</h3>
      <p>By clearing trees, digging waterholes and spreading seeds in their dung, elephants shape the land for other animals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Shrinking space</h3>
      <p>Habitat loss from farming and development is one of the biggest threats elephants face today.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
